## STEP 2 — TECHNICAL ANALYSIS

### FRONTEND

#### Component Structure
```
views/
├── layouts/boilerplate.ejs    ← Master layout (head, scripts, body wrapper)
├── includes/
│   ├── navbar.ejs             ← Navigation with search + auth dropdown
│   ├── footer.ejs             ← Static footer
│   └── flashMsg.ejs           ← Success/error alert dismissable
├── listings/
│   ├── index.ejs              ← Card grid of all listings
│   ├── show.ejs               ← Detail page + reviews + map
│   ├── new.ejs                ← Create form
│   └── edit.ejs               ← Edit form
├── users/
│   ├── signup.ejs             ← Registration form
│   └── login.ejs              ← Login form
└── error.ejs                  ← Error display page
```

#### Reusability: ⭐⭐ (2/5)
- [new.ejs](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/views/listings/new.ejs) and [edit.ejs](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/views/listings/edit.ejs) have **90% identical form markup** — should be a shared partial
- No shared form components, no shared card component
- Inline `<style>` blocks in [navbar.ejs](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/views/includes/navbar.ejs) and [index.ejs](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/views/listings/index.ejs) instead of CSS file

#### State Management: N/A (Server-rendered)
- No client-side state — all state lives in server sessions
- `currUser` passed as `res.locals` to every template — this is correct for EJS

#### API Handling Pattern
- **No API** — all interactions are traditional HTML form submissions with `method-override` for PUT/DELETE
- No AJAX, no fetch calls, no JSON endpoints

#### UI Logic vs Business Logic: ⭐⭐ (2/5)
- EJS templates contain business logic (price formatting, ownership checks, conditional rendering)
- [show.ejs](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/views/listings/show.ejs) serializes the entire listing object to JSON and exposes `MAP_TOKEN` in client-side script

> [!CAUTION]
> **Line 4 of show.ejs**: `const mapToken = "<%= process.env.MAP_TOKEN %>";` — This **exposes your Mapbox token directly in page source**. While Mapbox tokens are meant for client-side use, this pattern leaks server environment variables into the browser.

#### Performance Issues
1. **No lazy loading** for images — all listing images load eagerly
2. **No image optimization** — Cloudinary transformation only used in edit preview, not on index cards
3. **Mapbox GL JS + Geocoder loaded on every page** via boilerplate, even on pages without maps (index, new, edit, signup, login)
4. **Bootstrap + Font Awesome loaded from CDN** — no subset, full libraries

#### Responsiveness & UX Issues
- Cards have **fixed width `265px`** via inline style — breaks on some screen sizes
- Map has **fixed `500px` width** — overflows on mobile
- Show image has **fixed `500px × 320px`** — non-responsive
- Footer has hardcoded `margin-right: 600px` — breaks on smaller screens (partially fixed with media queries)
- CSS has **4 nearly identical media query blocks** that repeat the same styles
- Delete button visible to **all users** on reviews (no authorization check in template)

---

### BACKEND

#### Route Structure
| Method | Path | Purpose | Auth | Owner Check |
|--------|------|---------|------|-------------|
| GET | `/listings` | Index all | ❌ | ❌ |
| POST | `/listings` | Create | ✅ | ❌ |
| GET | `/listings/new` | New form | ✅ | ❌ |
| GET | `/listings/:id` | Show | ❌ | ❌ |
| PUT | `/listings/:id` | Update | ✅ | ✅ |
| DELETE | `/listings/:id` | Delete | ✅ | ✅ |
| GET | `/listings/:id/edit` | Edit form | ✅ | ✅ |
| POST | `/listings/:id/reviews` | Create review | ✅ | ❌ |
| DELETE | `/listings/:id/reviews/:reviewId` | Delete review | ✅ | ✅ (author) |
| GET/POST | `/signup` | Registration | ❌ | ❌ |
| GET/POST | `/login` | Login | ❌ | ❌ |
| GET | `/logout` | Logout | ❌ | ❌ |

#### Controller Logic: ⭐⭐⭐ (3/5)
- Controllers are thin enough — mostly CRUD operations
- **No service layer** — controllers directly call Mongoose and Mapbox
- [createListing](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/controllers/listing.js#30-47) does geocoding + image handling + model creation — too many responsibilities
- No input sanitization beyond Joi validation

#### Validation: ⭐⭐⭐ (3/5)
- Joi validates listing and review bodies ✅
- But validation **runs after file upload** (`upload.single()` comes before [validateListing](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/middleware.js#44-52) in route chain) — a malicious user can upload files even with invalid data
- No email format validation on signup
- No password strength requirements
- No duplicate email/username prevention beyond Mongoose unique errors

#### Error Handling: ⭐⭐⭐ (3/5)
- [wrapAsync](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/utils/wrapAsync.js#1-6) catches async errors ✅
- Global error handler renders error page ✅
- But [isOwner](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/middleware.js#24-33) middleware doesn't check if `listing` is null → **potential crash**
- [createListing](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/controllers/listing.js#30-47) doesn't check if geocoding returned results → `response.body.features[0]` can crash
- [createListing](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/controllers/listing.js#30-47) doesn't check if `req.file` exists → crash if no image uploaded

#### Security Issues

| Issue | Risk | Location |
|-------|------|----------|
| No CSRF tokens | High — form submissions can be forged | All forms |
| No `helmet.js` | Medium — missing security headers | [app.js](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/app.js) |
| No rate limiting | High — brute-force login, spam reviews | All routes |
| `saveUninitialized: true` | Low — creates sessions for anonymous users, wastes storage | `app.js:49` |
| No cookie `secure` flag | Medium — cookies sent over HTTP | `app.js:50-54` |
| No `sameSite` cookie attribute | Medium — cross-site request risks | `app.js:50-54` |
| XSS via `<%- JSON.stringify(listing) %>` | Medium — unescaped output | `show.ejs:5` |
| No file type/size validation on upload | Medium — oversized or malicious files accepted | Routes |

#### Auth Flow: ⭐⭐⭐ (3/5)
- Passport local strategy with `passport-local-mongoose` — simple but correct
- Auto-login after signup ✅
- Redirect URL preservation ✅
- But: no email verification, no password reset, no OAuth options

---

### DATABASE

#### Schema Design

```mermaid
erDiagram
    User {
        ObjectId _id PK
        String username UK
        String email
        String hash "auto by passport-local-mongoose"
        String salt "auto by passport-local-mongoose"
    }
    Listing {
        ObjectId _id PK
        String title
        String description
        Object image "url + filename"
        Number price
        String location
        String country
        Object geometry "GeoJSON Point"
        ObjectId owner FK
        ObjectId[] reviews FK
    }
    Review {
        ObjectId _id PK
        String comment
        Number rating "1-5"
        Date createdAt
        ObjectId author FK
    }
    
    User ||--o{ Listing : "owns"
    User ||--o{ Review : "authors" 
    Listing ||--o{ Review : "has"
```

#### Schema Quality: ⭐⭐ (2/5)

| Issue | Impact |
|-------|--------|
| **No indexes** beyond `_id` — no index on `owner`, `author`, `location`, `country` | Slow queries at scale |
| **No `timestamps: true`** on Listing schema | Can't sort by recency, no audit trail |
| Reviews stored as **embedded array of ObjectIds** — unbounded growth per listing | Performance degradation, 16MB doc limit risk |
| **No unique constraint on email** in User schema | Duplicate emails allowed |
| **No category/type field** on Listing — filter UI can never work | Feature gap |
| price has **no `min` constraint** in Mongoose schema (only in Joi) | Data integrity risk |
| **No text index** for search functionality | Search can never work |
| GeoJSON exists but **no `2dsphere` index** | Geospatial queries impossible |

#### Scalability Concerns
- `Listing.find({})` returns **all documents** — O(n) memory with no cursor/pagination
- Reviews embedded as ID array in Listing → array grows unboundedly
- No connection pooling configuration
- No read replicas or sharding strategy

---

### API DESIGN

#### REST Correctness: ⭐⭐⭐ (3/5)
- Standard RESTful routes for CRUD ✅
- Uses `method-override` for PUT/DELETE from HTML forms ✅
- Reviews nested under listings (`/listings/:id/reviews`) ✅
- But: no versioning, no content negotiation, no JSON API

#### Naming Consistency: ⭐⭐⭐⭐ (4/5)
- Resource naming is consistent and plural (`/listings`, `/reviews`)
- Auth routes use verbs (`/signup`, `/login`, `/logout`) — acceptable for HTML apps

#### Missing Endpoints
| Endpoint | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `GET /listings?search=` | Search by keyword |
| `GET /listings?category=` | Filter by category |
| `GET /listings?page=&limit=` | Pagination |
| `GET /users/:id` | User profile page |
| `PUT /users/:id` | Edit profile |
| `GET /listings/:id/reviews?page=` | Paginated reviews |
| `POST /listings/:id/book` | Booking (the core business action!) |
| `GET /api/listings` | JSON API for AJAX/mobile |

#### Overfetching
- `Listing.find({})` returns **all fields of all listings** — index page only needs title, image, price, location
- [showListing](file:///c:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Projects/wanderLust/controllers/listing.js#16-28) populates all reviews with full author objects — no pagination

